#!/usr/bin/env bash
# shellcheck extended-analysis=false
# (shellcheck's data-flow pass took 6.4 GB on this file, over the lander's 6 GB scope, so check-sh was OOM-killed;
# off, it is 0.2 GB and -S warning reports the same: it only drops info-level notes. 2026-10-13, #940/#914)
# cc-guard — PreToolUse hook: deterministic human gates for AUTONOMOUS and MEMBER-FACING sessions.
# Applies when CC_ROLE=worker|member is set, or the session cwd is under a dir marked .cc/track (a cc track
# worktree, role=worker) or .cc/member-facing (a session anybody in a Slack channel can talk to, role=member) —
# so interactive TRACK sessions are gated too (intended); plain operator/planning sessions are not.
# The MARKER is the truth: CC_ROLE is only a convenience, the walk works with no env at all.
# On top of every judge here, cc-guard-argv (next to this file) judges each Bash call on the argv bash would run, for
# the gated role, or as role pf for every other session but the permission seat (the hard limits no role is without:
# a reboot, a read of the box's credentials, a cc-slack member add). It only refuses: a call is refused when it or
# any judge here refuses, and nothing it answers lets through what a judge here refuses.
# Denies (exit 2 + reason) the four owner gates:
#   1. merge to default branch / deploy      2. external comms in owner's name
#   3. destructive / infra ops               4. (spend is enforced by --max-budget-usd on headless workers)
# …and three gates for EVERY session, gated role or not: nothing kills the shared tmux server (a tmux kill that ssh
# runs on another host is that host's, and passes: sshrc); no pattern kill (pkill,
# killall, or a pgrep/pidof feeding kill through xargs or $(…)) goes through while what it would take includes a session,
# a loop runner or the tmux server — read from the raw command, quotes and all, and resolved with pgrep at that moment;
# and nothing names a GitHub org on the box's deny list (~/.cc/github-deny — cc-github-deny wires the same list into git
# and gh) as an owner on a line that runs git, gh or reaches github.
# EVERY rule reads ONE anchor, so a word in front of a command turned all of them off at once (`timeout 5 tmux
# kill-server` walked past the universal gate). The haystack now carries each statement AND every suffix of it,
# plus what an option attaches after an `=` (`--filter=CMD` is run by a shell and is never its own word), so a
# rule sees the command wherever a wrapper put it — cmdpos(), which is also where that fix's ceiling is.
# The walk stops at a head with no option that runs a word ($NOEXEC), and at a SHELL carrying -n before its
# first operand: `bash -n <file>` and `shellcheck <file>` parse a script and run no line of it, so the filename
# after them is a file being read and not a command position. The kill fence reads which options carry a value
# PER VERB, because `-o` is a flag to pkill and `--older-than TIME` to killall.
# A NON-SHELL HEREDOC BODY IS READ FOR WHAT IT CAN RUN, NOT FOR THE WORDS IN IT. Unless some line of that body
# opens a door to a shell ($DOOR — the whole system/exec/spawn/popen/subprocess family, shell=True, child_process,
# a backtick in perl, ruby or php, qx, and a perl `open` with a pipe in it — or $PIPE, the pipe that IS the call in
# awk, perl, ruby and php: `print "x" | "<cmd>"` and `| getline` name no function at all), nothing in it can run a string, so
# the interior of its literals is data: a `.replace("<gated command>", …)` and a stub script in a trio-quoted string
# are not this shell doing what they name. The question is asked once per BODY, so a command built in a variable
# on one line and run on the next still has its door; and one door anywhere in the body has the whole of it read
# exactly as before, which is what keeps `os.system("<gated command>")` refused — hay(). What the SHELL expands
# is never dropped with the literal: under an unquoted heredoc delimiter a `$( )` inside a docstring has already
# run before the interpreter sees the body, so every substitution on the line stays in the haystack.
# WHAT RUNS UNDER ANOTHER NAME IS READ AS A COMMAND. A heredoc behind an expansion in command position (`$(cat <<EOF`)
# is run as the command; a git alias whose value starts with `!` and a file written by a redirect and then run by its
# path in the same command each go to the haystack as a statement (hands); and the words before a `"$(` heredoc
# substitution are read again with the tail after its terminator, so `cp "$(…)" <target>` keeps its target.
# A GLOB IN A NAME IS EVERY NAME IT CAN MATCH: a word holding [, ?, * or a {…} pair is read again as each command
# name it could expand to (deglob), so `cc-la[n]d` meets the rule `cc-land` does, in the gated tiers and in the three
# gates every session meets.
# OUTWARD AND IRREVERSIBLE DOORS ARE JUDGED ON ARGV (cc-guard-argv): merging, pushing to main, GitHub comms, landing,
# cc-try, the Slack doors, cc-publish, mail, deploys, root, containers, the host and network verbs and the box's
# credentials. The text rules left below are the ones that argv does not cover yet.
# READING A GATED TOOL IS NOT RUNNING IT. A sed whose script only prints, deletes, quits or counts by address is a
# reader like cat (sedread). The allowance is an ALLOW-LIST and the NARROW case: the sed read is HELD, not dropped, and unless EVERY statement of the command is itself a plain read with no
# substitution in it (reader, cmdpos END), the held text goes back in the haystack and is refused as before.
# A deny is one of TWO kinds. `deny` = the owner could say yes (merging, pushing, a comment in his name, an email,
# a post to his Slack); `never` = no yes exists for this tier (landing, dispatching a worker, host changes, the
# box's secrets). From a MEMBER-FACING session either RECORDS A QUERY the box's queries session drains and answers
# (record_query below, class page for a deny and nopage for a never) — nothing in the member's own channel, no page
# to the owner (owner, 2026-09-04). Writing outside the session's own tree is `never` too, but QUIET: the session
# aimed that write itself and reads the reason on stderr; not even a query is recorded for it.
# The Agent tool's `isolation: worktree` hands a subagent a tree of its own, and rule 5 FOLLOWS it there: a call
# standing in <checkout>/.claude/worktrees/<agent> writes THAT tree and no more of ~/dev around it ($awt, in_root)
# — when git says that dir is a LINKED WORKTREE, a mkdir'd one of the same name being the fence itself.
# Either gated tier also kills only its own descendants and a run it detached that stands in its own TRACK
# worktree (a member-facing dir is shared ground, and so is a cwd no marker named) — never a session, `-p` or
# interactive, a loop runner, a pane of the shared tmux, or anything it did not start (a member workspace's own
# workers run at role=member, so `worker` alone would miss them). Signal 0 in effect is a question, to a live
# pid or a gone one; any other signal to a gone pid is refused, since by the time it lands that pid is anybody's.
# EVERY gated role, worker included: no container runtime (this user is in group `docker`, which is root-equivalent)
# and no reading the files that ARE the box's credentials (~/ccbox/env and one login per harness: ~/.claude/.credentials.json,
# ~/.codex/auth.json, ~/.config/cursor/auth.json).
# role=member = every worker gate PLUS: no dispatching workers, no reading the box's other secrets, no re-wiring Slack.
# A MEMBER WORKSPACE (~/dev/<handle>/.cc/member-workspace, and any track of it) is the one exception: it dispatches
# workers and makes tracks INSIDE itself and nowhere else — docs/design-member-workspaces.md.
# It also gates whichever session is NOT live during an overlapping handoff (cc-handoff), by session id and
# by $CC_HANDOFF rather than by a marker: the two sessions share one cwd, so the walk below cannot tell them apart.
# A REFUSAL THAT FELL ON WORDS IS A FAILURE, AND IT IS RECORDED HERE: a rule that read text is re-read against a bare
# view of the command (quotes, heredoc bodies and comments out); if it no longer matches, the refusal cost the session
# a turn on permitted text and ONE line goes to the failure ledger (`cc-failures record`, kind
# guard-policy/permitted-text-refused, the command's shape and the rule, never its text). A correct refusal records
# nothing, and the write can never change the verdict — see note_refusal.
#   cc-guard selfcheck     the gates, against fixtures in a HOME of their own — never this box's state or sessions
# Env: CC_GUARD_ASKS, CC_GUARD_ASK_TTL (where a member-facing query is de-duplicated, and for how long) ·
# CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID (test hook: the session process the worker kill fence measures its subtree from).
# Env: CC_GUARD_RECORD_TIMEOUT (seconds the failure-ledger write in note_refusal may take; default 5; case 50c lowers it).
# Env: CC_GUARD_BUDGET (seconds a call may be read for before it is REFUSED; default 30, may only be lowered, never to 0).
# EVERY CALL IS READ INSIDE A BUDGET AND A BASH COMMAND PAST 128 KB IS REFUSED UNREAD: a hook that times out lets
# the call through, so running out of time refuses rather than racing the hook (see "the time budget").
set -uo pipefail
# No jq = no cwd, so no marker walk either: fail CLOSED on every role we can know from the env alone — `cc` sets it
# for a worker and a member. A gated role missing from this list is a role whose gates are silently OFF on a broken
# PATH, so "refused by cc-guard and not merely discouraged" has to hold on the day jq is not there either.
# sed IS ON THE LIST for the same reason: a rule that BLANKS a permitted spelling before it matches (cc-land --help,
# below) reads an empty line when sed is gone, and an empty line matches no rule — fail-open on the landing gate
# itself (security read of #574). Nothing else here may take a missing tool for a clean command.
for _dep in jq sed; do command -v "$_dep" >/dev/null 2>&1 && continue
  case "${CC_ROLE:-}${CC_HANDOFF:+ handoff}" in *worker*|*member*|*handoff*) echo "cc-guard: $_dep is missing (broken PATH?) — the gates cannot be evaluated, so the call is refused" >&2; exit 2;; esac; exit 0; done
# ---- the time budget ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A HOOK THAT TIMES OUT LETS THE CALL THROUGH. Tried live on claude 2.1.288 (2026-10-03): a PreToolUse hook that
# exits 2 after sleeping past its `timeout` did not stop the Bash call it was gating, and the same hook exiting 2 at
# once did. So a guard that reads slowly is a guard that can be talked past with a long enough command (the security
# read of #815 timed main at 160 s on a 40 KB single-quoted string, against the default 60 s timeout). Every call is
# therefore read under a budget of its own, well inside that timeout, and running out of it REFUSES — every role,
# because the gates for every session (the tmux server, pattern kills, the GitHub deny list) are inside the same
# read. timeout(1) signals its whole process group, so the awk and sed under the guard go with it. CC_GUARD_BUDGET
# (seconds) may only lower it, and never to 0, which is no timeout at all. The selfcheck lowers it to prove the
# refusal. The child is known by its env mark AND by timeout being its parent, so the mark alone in a session's env
# turns nothing off. ANY exit of 124 or more refuses: timeout's own failures (125-127) and a child killed by any
# signal are not a verdict. With no timeout on the PATH a gated role is refused, and any other session is read
# unbudgeted rather than not at all — the tmux, pattern-kill and GitHub gates are every session's. The second half
# of the bound is the size cap below.
if [ "${1:-}" != selfcheck ] \
   && ! { [ -n "${_CC_GUARD_TIMED:-}" ] && [ "$(cat "/proc/$PPID/comm" 2>/dev/null)" = timeout ]; }; then
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _b=${CC_GUARD_BUDGET:-30}
    [[ $_b =~ ^([0-9]{1,3})(\.[0-9]+)?$ ]] && (( 10#${BASH_REMATCH[1]} < 30 )) && [[ $_b =~ [1-9] ]] || _b=30
    _CC_GUARD_TIMED=1 timeout -k 2 "$_b" "$BASH" "$0" "$@"; _rc=$?
    [ "$_rc" -ge 124 ] && { echo "cc-guard: the gates did not finish reading this call inside ${_b}s, so it is" \
      "refused — a hook that runs out of time would let it through. Put long text in a file (Write tool) and pass" \
      "the file instead." >&2; exit 2; }
    exit "$_rc"
  fi
  case "${CC_ROLE:-}${CC_HANDOFF:+ handoff}" in *worker*|*member*|*handoff*)
    echo "cc-guard: timeout is missing (broken PATH?) — the gates cannot be read inside a budget, so the call is" \
      "refused" >&2; exit 2;; esac
fi
# ---- selfcheck -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It sits ABOVE the read of stdin because that read is where every other path here begins, and a hook has no
# other door. Each case builds its own fixture — its own HOME, its own asks dir, its own stub binaries — and
# asserts BOTH sides: what is refused AND what is still allowed, what pages AND what must not.
if [ "${1:-}" = selfcheck ]; then
  # The RUNNER's env decides nothing here: a worker running the gates exports CC_ROLE, and a member session its
  # CC_GUARD_ASKS — each case sets what it needs and reaches the child through `env` below.
  unset CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY CC_NOTIFY_LOG CC_ROLE CC_HANDOFF CC_HANDOFF_DIR CC_GUARD_ASKS CC_GUARD_ASK_TTL CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID CC_FAILURES
  # THE FIRST FIXTURE IS UNDER /tmp BY NAME, NOT UNDER $TMPDIR. Every case below was written against a $T in /tmp,
  # and cc now hands sessions TMPDIR=~/.cc/tmp (#639): there $T sat under /home, the recursive-delete text rule
  # matched every fixture path before the rule a case was asserting, and the three rm rows of the arch fence were
  # refused for the wrong reason. Where a fixture must sit OUTSIDE /tmp, that is $T2, on purpose.
  T=$(mktemp -d -p /tmp); H=$T/home; WT=$H/.cc/worktrees/demo/t1; n=0; f=0; pids=""
  # A SECOND FIXTURE HOME, AND THE POINT OF IT IS THAT IT IS NOT UNDER /tmp: the write-path rules wave /tmp
  # through as scratch, so a fixture there cannot test them at all — every path in $T is already allowed.
  # $HOME, because it is the one dir outside /tmp that exists in every place this runs; it goes with $T below.
  # Empty = case 12 FAILS rather than testing nothing: a fixture under /tmp would go green having gated nothing.
  T2=$(mktemp -d "$HOME/.cc-guard-selfcheck.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null) || T2=""
  endkids(){ local kp; for kp in "$@"; do pkill -P "$kp" 2>/dev/null; done; }   # a fixture script is sh + a sleep: end the sleep too. Fixtures sleep 600 and this trap ends them: at load 60 a case outlived a 45 s one and turned the kill fence cases red
  trap '[ -n "$pids" ] && { endkids $pids; kill $pids 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.2; kill -9 $pids 2>/dev/null; }; rm -rf "$T" ${T2:+"$T2"}' EXIT
  mkdir -p "$H/bin" "$H/.cc" "$H/dev/alice/.cc" "$H/dev/_cctest1/.cc" "$WT/.cc"
  : > "$H/dev/alice/.cc/member-facing"; : > "$H/dev/_cctest1/.cc/member-facing"; : > "$WT/.cc/track"
  # The owner's two real doors, stubbed: a case can then say "this was sent" and "nothing was".
  printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "notify $*" >> "$CALLS"\n' > "$H/bin/cc-notify"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\necho "slack $*" >> "$CALLS"\n' > "$H/bin/cc-slack"
  chmod +x "$H/bin/cc-notify" "$H/bin/cc-slack"
  # …and the failure ledger's door the same way: it logs each call and keeps the first call's arguments whole (NUL
  # between them) in $CALLS.args. It does NOT run the real writer: the guard gives that `timeout 5`, and on a loaded
  # box the write can outlast it, so a case reading the ledger went red on the box's speed (case 50a replays
  # the kept arguments through the real one itself, untimed). CC_FAILURES is exported for every case below all the
  # same, whichever binary a refusal finds: a refusal in any case here must never write the box's ledger.
  export CC_FAILURES=$T/ledger CC_MF_ROOT=$T/no-mfroot   # a test dir beside root's record, which mfroot always reads too: on a host where install.sh --etc has run, a repo it records reads as recorded here
  cat > "$H/bin/cc-failures" <<'FSTUB'
#!/bin/sh
echo "failures $*" >> "$CALLS"
[ -e "$CALLS.args" ] || printf '%s\000' "$@" > "$CALLS.args"
FSTUB
  chmod +x "$H/bin/cc-failures"
  # ...and a stand-in for a session process: `claude -p` is what the kill fence must never let go, and the
  # cases below need one they started themselves rather than a real session of this box's to point at.
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/claude"; chmod +x "$T/claude"
  ok(){ n=$((n+1)); if [ "$2" = 0 ]; then echo "  ✓ $1"; else f=$((f+1)); echo "  ✗ $1"; fi; }
  g(){ local t=$1 i=$2 c=$3 pl; shift 3   # a payload through the gate, in a HOME of its own; rc is the answer
    #  …and the REASON too, for a case that sets $GERR: rc 2 alone cannot say WHICH rule refused (case 11).
    #  …and the TRANSCRIPT PATH for a case that sets $GTP: the walk falls back to it when the cwd holds no marker,
    #  and a subagent's cwd never does — without it case 57 would anchor on the cwd and reproduce nothing.
    #  …and the SESSION and AGENT ids for a case that sets $GSID / $GAID: the handoff gate keys on both (case 69).
    pl=$(printf '{"tool_name":"%s","tool_input":%s,"cwd":"%s"%s%s%s}' "$t" "$i" "$c" "${GTP:+,\"transcript_path\":\"$GTP\"}" \
      "${GSID:+,\"session_id\":\"$GSID\"}" "${GAID:+,\"agent_id\":\"$GAID\"}")
    # A PAYLOAD THAT IS NOT JSON IS NOT A TEST. jq fails, the guard sees no tool at all and exits 0, so a row
    # asserting "still allowed" goes GREEN having gated nothing — one did, for a whole review round (#193).
    # rc 99 is neither 0 nor 2, so it fails whichever side the case is asserting.
    jq -e . >/dev/null 2>&1 <<<"$pl" || { echo 99; return; }
    printf '%s' "$pl" \
      | env HOME="${GH:-$H}" CALLS="$CALLS" CC_GUARD_ASKS="$ASKS" "$@" "$0" >/dev/null 2>"${GERR:-/dev/null}"; echo $?; }

  # 0. THE ARGV LAYER ONLY ADDS REFUSALS. Its own selfcheck passes; a reboot is refused for a session with no role and
  #    for a worker, and so is a worker's force push to main spelled as the argv reads it; a plain read still passes
  #    for both. A cc-guard-argv that crashes (exit 3) refuses a worker and lets the no-role `ls` through.
  CALLS=$T/calls0; ASKS=$T/asks0; mkdir -p "$T/x0"
  GA0=$(dirname "$(realpath "$0")")/cc-guard-argv
  "$GA0" selfcheck > "$T/ga0" 2>&1; ok "0 cc-guard-argv selfcheck ($(tail -1 "$T/ga0"))" $?
  for c in 'sudo reboot' 'sudo shutdown -h now' 'sudo poweroff' 'X=$(printf su%s do); $X re\"\"boot'; do
    r0=0; [ "$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$c\"}" "$T/x0")" = 2 ] || r0=1; ok "0 no role: $c refused" $r0
    r0=0; [ "$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$c\"}" "$WT")" = 2 ] || r0=1; ok "0 worker: $c refused" $r0
  done
  r0=0; [ "$(g Bash '{"command":"git -c remote.origin.push=HEAD:main push -f"}' "$WT")" = 2 ] || r0=1; ok "0 worker: push via remote.origin.push=HEAD:main refused" $r0
  r0=0; [ "$(g Bash '{"command":"ls -la"}' "$T/x0")" = 0 ] || r0=1; ok "0 no role: ls passes" $r0
  r0=0; [ "$(g Bash '{"command":"ls -la"}' "$WT")" = 0 ] || r0=1; ok "0 worker: ls passes" $r0
  mkdir -p "$T/fake0"; cp "$(realpath "$0")" "$T/fake0/cc-guard"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\n[ "$1" = judge ] && { echo boom >&2; exit 3; }\nexec "%s" "$@"\n' "$GA0" > "$T/fake0/cc-guard-argv"
  chmod +x "$T/fake0/cc-guard" "$T/fake0/cc-guard-argv"
  fk(){ printf '{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"ls"},"cwd":"%s"}' "$1" \
    | env HOME="$H" CALLS="$CALLS" CC_GUARD_ASKS="$ASKS" "$T/fake0/cc-guard" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?; }
  r0=0; [ "$(fk "$WT")" = 2 ] || r0=1; ok "0 argv exit 3: worker refused" $r0
  r0=0; [ "$(fk "$T/x0")" = 0 ] || r0=1; ok "0 argv exit 3: no role passes" $r0
  # …and a GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH in the caller's env never reaches the judge, since it would replace config/never-touch.txt
  # (perms' LAND on #1575). This judge refuses whenever it sees one, so `ls` passing for each tier shows it saw none.
  mkdir -p "$T/fake0n"; cp "$(realpath "$0")" "$T/fake0n/cc-guard"; : > "$T/nt0"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\n[ "$1" = judge ] && [ -n "${GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH+x}" ] && { printf "never\\tleaked\\n"; exit 0; }\nexec "%s" "$@"\n' "$GA0" > "$T/fake0n/cc-guard-argv"
  chmod +x "$T/fake0n/cc-guard" "$T/fake0n/cc-guard-argv"
  r0=0; [ "$(echo ls | GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH="$T/nt0" "$T/fake0n/cc-guard-argv" judge)" = "$(printf 'never\tleaked')" ] || r0=1
  ok "0 the leak-detecting judge refuses when it is handed GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH" $r0
  fn(){ printf '{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"ls"},"cwd":"%s"}' "$1" \
    | env HOME="$H" CALLS="$CALLS" CC_GUARD_ASKS="$ASKS" GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH="$T/nt0" "$T/fake0n/cc-guard" >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?; }
  r0=0; [ "$(fn "$WT")" = 0 ] || r0=1; ok "0 GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH in the caller's env: worker's judge never sees it" $r0
  r0=0; [ "$(fn "$T/x0")" = 0 ] || r0=1; ok "0 GUARD_NEVER_TOUCH in the caller's env: no role's judge never sees it" $r0

  # 1. A MEMBER REFUSAL IS A QUERY, NOT A LINE IN THE MEMBER'S CHANNEL. The landing tool from a member-facing
  #    session: refused, a query RECORDED for the box's queries session, and NOTHING in the member's own channel
  #    and NOTHING paged to the owner (owner, 2026-09-04). cc-land has no yes for a member, so class=nopage.
  ASKS=$T/asks1; CALLS=$T/calls1; : > "$CALLS"; QS=$H/.cc/state/alice/queries.spool; rm -f "$QS"
  rc=$(g Bash '{"command":"cc-land 7"}' "$H/dev/alice")
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ] && [ ! -s "$CALLS" ] && [ "$(wc -l < "$QS" 2>/dev/null)" = 1 ] \
     && jq -e 'select(.class=="nopage" and .workspace=="alice" and (.command|test("cc-land")))' "$QS" >/dev/null 2>&1; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a member refusal records a query for the box and says nothing in the member's channel or to the owner" $r

  # 2. …AND AN APPROVABLE ONE IS RECORDED ONCE. gh pr merge could be yes, so class=page — but a member retrying the
  #    same blocked command must not fill the spool: one query per workspace+reason inside the TTL.
  ASKS=$T/asks2; CALLS=$T/calls2; : > "$CALLS"; QS=$H/.cc/state/alice/queries.spool; rm -f "$QS"
  rc=$(g Bash '{"command":"gh pr merge 1"}' "$H/dev/alice")
  rc2=$(g Bash '{"command":"gh pr merge 1"}' "$H/dev/alice")
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ] && [ "$rc2" = 2 ] && [ ! -s "$CALLS" ] && [ "$(wc -l < "$QS")" = 1 ] \
     && jq -e 'select(.class=="page")' "$QS" >/dev/null 2>&1; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "an approvable member refusal is recorded as one page-class query, and a retry inside the TTL does not record it again" $r

  # 3. THE QUERY NAMES THE WORKSPACE, THE COMMAND AND THE REASON — the three things the queries session reads to
  #    approve or answer it, since a member sandbox cannot reach the owner itself and the host drains this spool.
  ASKS=$T/asks3; CALLS=$T/calls3; : > "$CALLS"; QS=$H/.cc/state/alice/queries.spool; rm -f "$QS"
  rc=$(g Bash '{"command":"gh pr merge 42"}' "$H/dev/alice")
  named=$(jq -er 'select(.workspace=="alice" and (.reason|test("merging")) and (.command|test("gh pr merge 42")))|"y"' "$QS" 2>/dev/null)
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ] && [ "$named" = y ] && [ ! -s "$CALLS" ]; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "the recorded query names the workspace, the command and the reason the queries session needs to act on it" $r

  # 4. A WORKER KILLS ONLY ITS OWN. Its child goes; the pid of something it did not start does not (11:15Z:
  #    a worker pgrep'd the suite's filename, matched its own session and ended its own iteration).
  ASKS=$T/asks4; CALLS=$T/calls4; : > "$CALLS"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & child=$!; pids="$pids $child"
  own=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill $child\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  out=$(g Bash '{"command":"kill -9 1"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$own" = 0 ] && [ "$out" = 2 ] && [ ! -s "$CALLS" ]; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a worker kills its own child, is refused a pid outside its subtree, and pages nobody either way" $r

  # 4b. A NEGATIVE PID IS A PROCESS GROUP. `kill -9 -1` parsed as two signals and was waved through, which is
  #     every process this user owns; a named group is fenced pid by pid like any other target.
  ASKS=$T/asks4b; CALLS=$T/calls4b; : > "$CALLS"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & c2=$!; pids="$pids $c2"
  all=$(g Bash '{"command":"kill -9 -1"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  grp=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill -TERM -$(ps -o pgid= -p $c2 | tr -d ' ')\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  sig=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill -9 $c2\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$all" = 2 ] && [ "$sig" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$CALLS" ]; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a worker is refused \`kill -9 -1\`, keeps its leading signal, and a named group is fenced pid by pid (rc $grp)" $r

  # 5. …and a session process is off limits even INSIDE the subtree: the incident was a worker killing the
  #    `claude -p` it was running in, which is its own ancestor, but a child one would end the run just as dead.
  "$T/claude" -p x >/dev/null 2>&1 & held=$!; pids="$pids $held"
  sleep 0.3
  rc=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill $held\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ]; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a worker is refused a kill of a claude -p, subtree or not" $r

  # 5b. …and the fence is the MEMBER tier's too, which is where it is needed most: a worker dispatched inside
  #     a member workspace runs at role=member (track_dir: the repo decides the tier, not the worktree), and
  #     that is the session running `claude -p` in a loop — the one the 11:15Z incident happened to. Its own
  #     pair of processes and its own call log, and the refusal must not page either: a kill is not a thing
  #     the owner could say yes to.
  ASKS=$T/asks5b; CALLS=$T/calls5b; : > "$CALLS"
  "$T/claude" -p x >/dev/null 2>&1 & mheld=$!; pids="$pids $mheld"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & mchild=$!; pids="$pids $mchild"
  sleep 0.3
  rc=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill $mheld\"}" "$H/dev/alice" CC_ROLE=member CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  out=$(g Bash '{"command":"kill -9 1"}' "$H/dev/alice" CC_ROLE=member CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  own=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill $mchild\"}" "$H/dev/alice" CC_ROLE=member CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$rc" = 2 ] && [ "$out" = 2 ] && [ "$own" = 0 ] && ! grep -q '^notify' "$CALLS"; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "the same fence holds at the member tier, where a member workspace's own workers run — and refusing a kill pages nobody" $r

  # 6. A PATTERN IS RESOLVED, NOT GUESSED. One marked process, asked about twice: from a root that did not
  #    start it the pkill is refused, from the root that did it is allowed. The root is a pid this case picks,
  #    so the answer comes from the fixture and not from whatever else on the box happens to match.
  gk=marked$$                                   # minted per run, for the reason case 7 spells out
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$gk"; chmod +x "$T/$gk"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & bare=$!; pids="$pids $bare"   # a root that started nothing: the marked process is outside it
  "$T/$gk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & mark=$!; pids="$pids $mark"  # ...and inside this shell's, the other root below
  sleep 0.3
  hit=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -f $gk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$bare)
  mine=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -f $gk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  miss=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -f $gk-no-such-process-here\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$bare)
  if [ "$hit" = 2 ] && [ "$mine" = 0 ] && [ "$miss" = 0 ] && kill -0 "$mark" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a worker's pkill -f is refused when it resolves outside its subtree, allowed when it resolves inside it, and allowed when it resolves to nothing" $r

  # 6b. A PATTERN HIT GONE BEFORE THE FENCE LOOKS IS NOTHING TO REFUSE (10-30: a member's `pkill -f build_site.py`, with no
  #     such process, refused twice on a pid that had already vanished). A pgrep on PATH reports a pid this case reaped,
  #     for this pattern alone; the pkill, the xargs hunt and the substitution pass, and a kill naming that pid is still refused.
  gm=gone$$; mkdir -p "$T/pg6b"; sleep 0 & gp=$!; wait "$gp"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$*" in *%s*) echo %s;; *) exec /usr/bin/pgrep "$@";; esac\n' "$gm" "$gp" > "$T/pg6b/pgrep"; chmod +x "$T/pg6b/pgrep"
  r=0
  for x in "pkill -f $gm" "pgrep -f $gm | xargs kill" "kill \$(pgrep -f $gm)"; do
    [ "$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$x\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$ PATH="$T/pg6b:$PATH")" = 0 ] || { r=1; echo "      $x was refused on a hit that had gone"; }
  done
  [ "$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill $gp\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$ PATH="$T/pg6b:$PATH")" = 2 ] || { r=1; echo "      kill of the gone pid itself was let through"; }
  ok "a pattern kill whose pgrep hit has gone by the time the fence looks passes, while a kill naming that gone pid is still refused" $r

  # 7. HUNT, THEN KILL. `pgrep -f <name> | xargs kill -9` is what the worker actually typed on 11:15Z, and
  #    it is the shape the target loop cannot see: the kill carries no pid of its own. Its own marked
  #    process, asked about from both roots, plus a feed that is no pgrep at all. The mark is minted per run
  #    ($$) because a fixed word here matched the shell that invoked this file — a command line carrying the
  #    word is exactly how the incident happened, and a case must not be decided by one.
  hk=hunted$$                                              # a name minted for this run: see below
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$hk"; chmod +x "$T/$hk"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & hbare=$!; pids="$pids $hbare"   # a root that started nothing
  "$T/$hk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & hm=$!; pids="$pids $hm"
  sleep 0.3
  ASKS=$T/asks7; CALLS=$T/calls7; : > "$CALLS"
  hit=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pgrep -f $hk | xargs kill -9\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  mine=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pgrep -f $hk | xargs kill -9\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  opt=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pgrep -f $hk | xargs -n 1 kill -9\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  blind=$(g Bash '{"command":"cat pidfile | xargs kill -9"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  sub=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"kill -9 \$(pgrep -f $hk)\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  if [ "$hit" = 2 ] && [ "$mine" = 0 ] && [ "$opt" = 2 ] && [ "$blind" = 2 ] && [ "$sub" = 2 ] \
     && kill -0 "$hm" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a worker hunting with pgrep and killing through xargs is fenced on what the pgrep finds — with an option between the two, refused outside its subtree, allowed inside it, and refused when the feed is not a pgrep at all" $r

  # 7b. …AND THROUGH A VARIABLE (10-28: a builder waiting on its own checks took the lander's unittest run from
  #     pgrep and killed it, ending a 66 min gate). The variable cannot be read, so the pgrep that fills it is.
  hk=varhunted$$
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$hk"; chmod +x "$T/$hk"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & hbare=$!; pids="$pids $hbare"
  "$T/$hk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & hm=$!; pids="$pids $hm"
  sleep 0.3
  vset=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"P=\$(pgrep -f $hk); kill \$P\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  vfor=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"for p in \$(pgrep -f $hk); do kill -9 \$p; done\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  vmine=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"for p in \$(pgrep -f $hk); do kill -9 \$p; done\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  vjob=$(g Bash '{"command":"sleep 600 & kill $!"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$hbare)
  if [ "$vset" = 2 ] && [ "$vfor" = 2 ] && [ "$vmine" = 0 ] && [ "$vjob" = 0 ] && kill -0 "$hm" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a kill of a variable a pgrep fills is fenced on what the pgrep finds, by assignment or for loop, while a variable with no pgrep beside it (kill \$!) passes" $r

  # 8. AN OPTION'S VALUE IS NOT THE PATTERN. `pkill --signal TERM <name>` read TERM as the pattern, matched
  #    nothing and went through — and TERMs every match of the real name, this session included.
  ASKS=$T/asks8; CALLS=$T/calls8; : > "$CALLS"
  sk=signalled$$                                           # its own mark and its own pair, as above
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$sk"; chmod +x "$T/$sk"
  sleep 600 >/dev/null 2>&1 & sbare=$!; pids="$pids $sbare"
  "$T/$sk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & sm=$!; pids="$pids $sm"
  sleep 0.3
  sig=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill --signal TERM -f $sk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$sbare)
  usr=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -u $(id -u) -f $sk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$sbare)
  own=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill --signal TERM -f $sk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  none=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill --signal TERM -f $sk-no-such-process-here\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$sbare)
  if [ "$sig" = 2 ] && [ "$usr" = 2 ] && [ "$own" = 0 ] && [ "$none" = 0 ] && kill -0 "$sm" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "an option's value is never mistaken for the pattern: --signal and -u are consumed, so the name after them is what gets resolved" $r

  # 9. A SELECTOR WITH NO PATTERN IS STILL EVERY PROCESS. `pkill -u $(id -u)` parses to no pattern at all, so
  #    the branch that resolves patterns found nothing to fence and waved it through — every process this user
  #    owns, this session's own claude -p and loop runner included, which is `kill -9 -1` spelled a second way
  #    (review of #181). Now refused, and refused for the attached and long spellings too, which is why this
  #    fails closed rather than resolving: the matcher cannot be taught every one. Both sides, as ever — the
  #    same selector carrying a pattern still resolves the pattern, and its own marked process is still there
  #    afterwards. The root here is the one that DID start the mark, so what refuses is the missing pattern
  #    and not the subtree test. Mark minted per run ($$) for the reason case 7 spells out.
  ASKS=$T/asks9; CALLS=$T/calls9; : > "$CALLS"
  nk=selected$$
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$nk"; chmod +x "$T/$nk"
  "$T/$nk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & nm=$!; pids="$pids $nm"
  sleep 0.3
  uid=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -u $(id -u)\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  pgr=$(g Bash '{"command":"pkill -g 1"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)   # group 1: nothing this shell started
  att=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -u$(id -u)\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  kal=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"killall -u $(id -un)\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  own=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -u $(id -u) -f $nk\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$uid" = 2 ] && [ "$pgr" = 2 ] && [ "$att" = 2 ] && [ "$kal" = 2 ] && [ "$own" = 0 ] \
     && kill -0 "$nm" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "a pkill that selects a set but names no pattern is refused rather than resolved — -u, -g, an attached -u<uid> and killall -u alike — while the same selector carrying a pattern still resolves that pattern" $r

  # 10. …and the selectors that were still resolvable TO GARBAGE. `--older 3600` and `-F <pidfile>` take a
  #     value, so an argument list that skipped it read the value as the pattern, `pgrep -- 3600` matched
  #     nothing, and the fence found nothing to refuse — the same hole as `--signal TERM`, one round later.
  #     Its own mark and its own root, and the third line is the half that must NOT change: a selector
  #     carrying a real pattern still resolves that pattern.
  ASKS=$T/asks10; CALLS=$T/calls10; : > "$CALLS"
  ok10=selectors$$
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$ok10"; chmod +x "$T/$ok10"
  "$T/$ok10" >/dev/null 2>&1 & om=$!; pids="$pids $om"
  : > "$T/pidfile10"
  sleep 0.3
  old=$(g Bash '{"command":"pkill --older 3600"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  pf=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -F $T/pidfile10\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  qv=$(g Bash '{"command":"pkill -q 7"}' "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  # …and `-O`, --older's short spelling, which was the one still open: with 3600 read as the pattern the -u
  # behind it never reached the fence, so this selected every process of that uid and matched none of them.
  sho=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -O 3600 -u $(id -u)\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  keep=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill --older 3600 -f $ok10\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  shk=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"pkill -O 3600 -f $ok10\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$)
  if [ "$old" = 2 ] && [ "$pf" = 2 ] && [ "$qv" = 2 ] && [ "$sho" = 2 ] && [ "$keep" = 0 ] && [ "$shk" = 0 ] \
     && kill -0 "$om" 2>/dev/null; then r=0; else r=1; fi
  ok "an option's value is not a pattern however it is spelled: --older, its short -O, -F and -q consume theirs and are refused for naming no process, while the same option carrying a pattern still resolves it" $r

  # 10b. …AND WHAT A LETTER MEANS DEPENDS ON THE VERB. One parser serves pgrep, pkill and killall, and they
  #      disagree about the same short option: `-o` is --oldest (a FLAG) to pkill and --older-than TIME (a
  #      VALUE) to killall, `-n` is --newest against --ns PID. Read as pkill's, killall's value became the
  #      pattern — `killall --older-than 1h claude` reduced to pat=1h, `pgrep -x -- 1h` found nothing, the
  #      fence had nothing to refuse and it went through, killing every claude over an hour old: this
  #      worker's own claude -p and its loop runner (review of #193). Its own claude stand-in, started here
  #      rather than borrowed from case 9, so this row cannot pass on a process another case left running.
  ASKS=$T/asks10b; CALLS=$T/calls10b; : > "$CALLS"; GERR=$T/err10b
  # …and it carries a mark that is in its COMMAND LINE and not in its name, which is what tells a walk that
  # kept `-f` from one that ate it: `pgrep -f -- <mark>` finds this process, plain `pgrep -- <mark>` does not.
  mk=mark10b$$
  "$T/claude" -p "$mk" >/dev/null 2>&1 & kc=$!; pids="$pids $kc"
  # …and a second stand-in that is nothing but its own name, for the rows that must stay ALLOWED: it has to
  # resolve to a process this case started, so "allowed" means the fence ran and judged it, not that the
  # pattern matched nothing. Its own, not case 10's — a row that reads another case's fixture passes on
  # whatever that case happened to leave running.
  own=own10b$$
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 600\n' > "$T/$own"; chmod +x "$T/$own"
  "$T/$own" >/dev/null 2>&1 & ko=$!; pids="$pids $ko"
  sleep 0.3
  k(){ : > "$GERR"; g Bash "{\"command\":\"$1\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$; }
  r=0
  # The incident itself: the value is consumed, so the NAME behind it is what reaches the fence. What is
  # asserted is the pattern the refusal NAMES — `(killall claude)`, not `(killall 1h)` — because WHICH pid
  # it stops at, and so whether the reason reads "session or a loop runner" or "outside this worker's own
  # process subtree", depends on what else is running on the box; the resolved pattern does not.
  [ "$(k "killall --older-than 1h claude")" = 2 ] && grep -qF '(killall claude)' "$GERR" \
    || { r=1; echo "      killall --older-than 1h claude: refused for: $(head -c 60 "$GERR")"; }
  # …and the same command with nothing left to name: no pattern at all, which is the other refusal.
  while read -r sel; do
    [ "$(k "killall $sel")" = 2 ] && grep -qF 'selector but no pattern' "$GERR" \
      || { r=1; echo "      killall $sel: refused for: $(head -c 60 "$GERR")"; }
  done <<'K10B'
--older-than 1h
-o 1h
-y 1h
-n 1234
-Z ctx
K10B
  # THE HALF THAT MUST NOT MOVE, and the reason the verb is passed in rather than the options merged: to
  # pkill those same letters are flags, so merging would eat the word after them. `pkill -o <pattern>` would
  # reduce to no pattern and be refused, and worse, `pgrep -o -f <x>` would lose its -f and resolve without
  # --full — fewer processes found, nothing fenced, a refusal turned into a pass.
  for flag in -o -n; do
    { [ "$(k "pkill $flag $own")" = 0 ] && kill -0 "$ko" 2>/dev/null; } \
      || { r=1; echo "      pkill $flag <own child> was refused: $(head -c 60 "$GERR")"; }
  done
  # …and this is that harm, made to show: the mark is findable only with --full, so a walk that ate the `-f`
  # resolves nothing, fences nothing and lets the kill through. It must stay REFUSED.
  [ "$(k "pgrep -o -f $mk | xargs kill -9")" = 2 ] \
    || { r=1; echo "      pgrep -o -f <a claude -p> | xargs kill was allowed through"; }
  { kill -0 "$kc" 2>/dev/null && kill -0 "$ko" 2>/dev/null; } || { r=1; echo "      a stand-in did not survive the case"; }
  ok "a short option means what its own verb says: killall's -o/-y/-n/-Z take a value, so the name behind them still reaches the fence, while pkill's -o/-n stay flags and the pattern after them is still resolved" $r

  # 11. A WRAPPER WORD IS NOT A WAY PAST A GATE, AND THE ANCHOR IS ONE VARIABLE. `$AT` knew a fixed set of
  #     wrappers, so any word outside it hid the command behind it — and $AT is interpolated into every rule
  #     this file has, so that was ONE hole in all of them and not a hole in two. Measured 2026-09-02:
  #     `pkill -f claude` refused and `timeout 5 pkill -f claude` allowed; `tmux kill-server` refused and
  #     `setsid tmux kill-server` allowed — the shared tmux server every session on this box runs inside,
  #     from any session, by prefixing one word. So the table below is one row per rule FAMILY, each in a
  #     wrapper $AT never knew, and each must be REFUSED. Its own fixture: its own asks dir, its own call log,
  #     and the handoff row its own empty record dir. The tiers: un = no role and no marker at all, which is
  #     what makes the tmux rows the universal gate rather than a worker one.
  ASKS=$T/asks11; CALLS=$T/calls11; : > "$CALLS"; mkdir -p "$T/hd11"; GERR=$T/err11
  r=0
  # Each row asserts the REASON as well as rc 2, because rc 2 alone would stay green if some neighbouring rule
  # happened to refuse the row — and the claim here is that the wrapped command reached ITS OWN rule.
  while IFS='|' read -r fam tier why what; do
    [ -n "$fam" ] || continue
    : > "$GERR"                                        # …and no row may pass on the reason a previous one left
    case "$tier" in
      un) got=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$what\"}" "$T");;
      hg) got=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$what\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$ CC_HANDOFF=h11 CC_HANDOFF_DIR="$T/hd11");;
      mb) got=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$what\"}" "$H/dev/_cctest1" CC_ROLE=member CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$);;
      *)  got=$(g Bash "{\"command\":\"$what\"}" "$WT" CC_ROLE=worker CC_GUARD_ROOT_PID=$$);;
    esac
    { [ "$got" = 2 ] && grep -qF "$why" "$GERR"; } \
      || { r=1; echo "      $fam: rc $got, refused for: $(head -c 60 "$GERR")"; }
  done <<W11
tmux, the universal gate|un|shared tmux server|timeout 5 tmux kill-server
tmux, a pathed wrapper|un|shared tmux server|/usr/bin/setsid tmux kill-server
tmux, a glob in the name, from a session with no role|un|shared tmux server|/usr/bin/tmu[x] kill-server
…and a ? in it|un|shared tmux server|/usr/bin/tmu? kill-server
…and a glob past the cap, read as the floor's names alone|un|shared tmux server|/usr/bin/[a-u]* kill-server
tmux, a shell keyword|un|shared tmux server|! nice -n 5 tmux kill-server
tmux, systemd-run|un|shared tmux server|systemd-run --user tmux kill-session
tmux, an interpreter running its quoted string|un|shared tmux server|bash -c 'timeout 5 tmux kill-server'
an option that runs its value, spelled as two tokens|un|shared tmux server|man -P 'tmux kill-server' ls
…and the same option spelled --opt=VALUE|un|shared tmux server|sort --compress-program='tmux kill-server'
a search tool with an option that runs a word|un|shared tmux server|rg --pre 'tmux kill-server' x
…the same one, attached|un|shared tmux server|rg --pre='tmux kill-server' x
…and its two neighbours, one option shape each|wt|landing a change|ag --pager cc-land x
…attached|wt|landing a change|ack --pager=cc-land x
a downloader that runs an option's value|wt|landing a change|wget --use-askpass=cc-land http://x
--opt=VALUE whose value is one bare word|wt|landing a change|sort --compress-program=cc-land
a key=value carried in an option's own token|un|shared tmux server|git -c core.pager='tmux kill-server' log
merge|wt|merging a PR|nice -n 5 gh pr merge 3
land|wt|landing a change|stdbuf -o0 cc-land 7
putting a PR first in its lane|wt|landing a change|cc-land queue demo 7 --first
deploy|wt|deploying (|ionice vercel deploy --prod
public github comms|wt|posting to a PR or issue|chrt -f 1 gh issue create -t x
raw gh api|wt|raw gh api call|taskset -c 0 gh api -X DELETE /user
email|wt|sending email|flock /tmp/l11 mail -s x owner@example.com
the owner's slack link|wt|cc slack off|watch cc slack off
posting as the owner|mb|posting to the owner's Slack|torify cc-slack post -c #x hi
a wrapper onto the same inject door|wt|typing into a session's prompt|torify cc-broker inject planner hi
the ledger door onto that same path|mb|typing into a session's prompt|cc-broker ledger planner hi
the document upload onto that same door|mb|posting to the owner's Slack|nohup cc-arch landed myrepo
the publish that gives it its turn|wt|publishing the box's code outward|nice -n 5 cc-publish
…and with a message of its own|mb|publishing the box's code outward|cc-publish -m 'x'
a TAB where the rule spelled a space|mb|posting to the owner's Slack|cc-slack\tpost -c #x hi
…and a tab in front of the command too|wt|typing into a session's prompt|\tcc-broker\tinject planner hi
write path, over the ledger of what was sent outward|wt|writing into the box's live tree|cp /tmp/x11 $H/.cc/state/arch/myrepo.posted
…and a redirect into the same directory|wt|writing into the box's live tree|echo x > $H/.cc/state/arch/myrepo.cursor
…and DELETING it, which suppresses a revision instead of repeating one|wt|removing the record of what has already been sent outward|rm $H/.cc/state/arch/myrepo.cursor
…the whole directory at once|wt|removing the record of what has already been sent outward|rm -rf $H/.cc/state/arch
…or moving it out of the way|wt|removing the record of what has already been sent outward|mv $H/.cc/state/arch/myrepo.posted /tmp/x11
…or emptying it in place|wt|removing the record of what has already been sent outward|truncate -s 0 $H/.cc/state/arch/myrepo.posted
